From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel <daniel@matv.io>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 00:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] debayer_cpu: subtract the black level before applying gains

PEDESTAL-AMPLIFICATION BUG (upstream candidate, affects every sensor
with a non-zero black level on the CPU soft ISP): updateLookupTables()
built the per-channel LUTs as
    LUT[i] = gammaTable_[clamp(gain_ch * i * 4, 0, 1023)]
i.e. the white balance gain multiplied the raw value INCLUDING the
sensor's black pedestal. The gamma table's zero-point
(blackIndex = blackLevel[green] * 1024, flat-filled below) only blacks
out indices < blackIndex, so for green (gain 1.0) the pedestal lands
in the flat fill, but for R/B (gains 1.5-2.7 in dim scenes on fajita's
IMX376) the pedestal maps well above the zero-point and renders as a
violet wash over every dark region. Field data after the stats-offset
fix (patch 12): dim-scene channel means R90/G55/B100 -- dark areas are
lifted only in the high-gain channels. Real ISPs subtract the black
level BEFORE applying WB gains; libcamera's own GPU debayer does too
(bayer_unpacked.frag / bayer_1x_packed.frag: "rgb = rgb - blacklevel"
before the ccm multiply). The CPU LUTs are the odd one out.

Fix, gains-only (non-CCM) path: build the index as
    zeroPoint + gain_ch * (i * 4 - blackIndex_ch)
clamped to [0, 1023] (float arithmetic, so no signed/unsigned traps;
under-black results land in the flat fill / at 0 which renders as
black). Signal above black gets the gain; the pedestal maps to the
zero-point for ALL channels regardless of gain. blackIndex is floored
per channel exactly like updateGammaTable() truncates its zero-point,
which makes gain 1.0 a bit-exact identity with the old code and the
pedestal -> zeroPoint mapping exact. With blackLevel == 0 the new
expression degenerates to the old one. The black level is indexed per
channel so a future per-channel BLC (dark-frame calibration in
blc.cpp, DebayerParams::blackLevel is already RGB<float>) works here
unmodified; the per-channel values must follow the same colour
indexing convention as params.gains (the swapRedBlueGains_ table
aliasing then handles R/B-swapped formats for both equally).

Fix, CCM path: the combined matrix has the WB gains folded in
(combinedMatrix = ccm * gainMatrix), so it amplified the pedestal the
same way, PLUS leaked it across channels through the off-diagonal
terms. Correct form is out = M * (in - black) + greenZero, where
greenZero is the green channel's 8-bit black level -- the same shared
zero-point updateGammaTable() derives from green and that the
gains-only path re-anchors every channel to. Restoring each channel's
own pedestal instead would, once per-channel black levels differ
(dark-frame BLC calibration), put an R/B pedestal above green's back
above the gamma zero-point where it survives as tinted blacks -- the
defect class this patch removes. Only the re-anchor constant is
shared-green; the subtracted M * black keeps the true per-channel
vector. The constant greenZero - M * black is folded into the green
table's entries since every output pixel sums exactly one entry from
each of the three tables. All-pedestal input now maps every channel
exactly to the green 8-bit black level (gammaLut_'s zero-point), with
uniform per-channel blacks the correction is bit-identical to
restoring the per-channel level, saturating whites are unchanged
after the [0,255] clamp, and a zero black level yields a zero
correction (no behaviour change). This keeps the CPU CCM path
consistent with the GPU shader path.
---
 src/libcamera/software_isp/debayer_cpu.cpp | 58 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 54 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

--- a/src/libcamera/software_isp/debayer_cpu.cpp
+++ b/src/libcamera/software_isp/debayer_cpu.cpp
@@ -803,13 +803,40 @@ void DebayerCpu::updateLookupTables(DebayerParams &params)
 			const unsigned int redIndex = swapRedBlueGains_ ? 2 : 0;
 			const unsigned int greenIndex = 1;
 			const unsigned int blueIndex = swapRedBlueGains_ ? 0 : 2;
+			/*
+			 * As in the gains-only path below, the black level must
+			 * not go through the colour matrix (which has the white
+			 * balance gains folded in): compute
+			 * out = M * (in - black) + greenZero instead of M * in,
+			 * where greenZero is the green channel's 8-bit black
+			 * level -- the shared zero-point updateGammaTable()
+			 * derives from green.  Re-anchoring every output channel
+			 * there (instead of restoring each channel's own
+			 * pedestal) keeps unequal per-channel black levels from
+			 * landing above the gamma zero-point and surviving as
+			 * tinted blacks; the gains-only path anchors at the
+			 * green zero-point the same way.  The constant
+			 * greenZero - M * black is folded into the green table,
+			 * since each output pixel sums exactly one entry of
+			 * each of the three tables.  The GPU debayer already
+			 * subtracts the black level before the matrix
+			 * (bayer_unpacked.frag); the CPU tables did not.
+			 */
+			const RGB<float> blackLevel8 =
+				params.blackLevel * static_cast<float>(kRGBLookupSize - 1);
+			const RGB<float> greenZero8(blackLevel8[greenIndex]);
+			const RGB<float> ccmBlack =
+				greenZero8 - params.combinedMatrix * blackLevel8;
 			for (unsigned int i = 0; i < kRGBLookupSize; i++) {
 				red[i].r = std::round(i * params.combinedMatrix[redIndex][0]);
 				red[i].g = std::round(i * params.combinedMatrix[greenIndex][0]);
 				red[i].b = std::round(i * params.combinedMatrix[blueIndex][0]);
-				green[i].r = std::round(i * params.combinedMatrix[redIndex][1]);
-				green[i].g = std::round(i * params.combinedMatrix[greenIndex][1]);
-				green[i].b = std::round(i * params.combinedMatrix[blueIndex][1]);
+				green[i].r = std::round(i * params.combinedMatrix[redIndex][1] +
+							ccmBlack[redIndex]);
+				green[i].g = std::round(i * params.combinedMatrix[greenIndex][1] +
+							ccmBlack[greenIndex]);
+				green[i].b = std::round(i * params.combinedMatrix[blueIndex][1] +
+							ccmBlack[blueIndex]);
 				blue[i].r = std::round(i * params.combinedMatrix[redIndex][2]);
 				blue[i].g = std::round(i * params.combinedMatrix[greenIndex][2]);
 				blue[i].b = std::round(i * params.combinedMatrix[blueIndex][2]);
@@ -822,9 +849,32 @@ void DebayerCpu::updateLookupTables(DebayerParams &params)
 			auto &red = swapRedBlueGains_ ? blue_ : red_;
 			auto &green = green_;
 			auto &blue = swapRedBlueGains_ ? red_ : blue_;
+			/*
+			 * The white balance gains must only amplify the signal
+			 * above the black level; the sensor pedestal carries no
+			 * light information.  Multiplying the raw value
+			 * directly maps the pedestal of any channel with
+			 * gain > 1 above the gamma table zero-point, lifting
+			 * dark regions of the high-gain channels (a
+			 * violet/magenta cast in dim scenes).  Subtract the
+			 * (per-channel capable) black level before applying
+			 * the gain, so the black level itself maps to the
+			 * zero-point for every channel regardless of its gain.
+			 */
+			/* Truncate like the updateGammaTable() zero-point */
+			const RGB<float> blackIndex = { {
+				std::floor(params.blackLevel[0] * gammaTableSize),
+				std::floor(params.blackLevel[1] * gammaTableSize),
+				std::floor(params.blackLevel[2] * gammaTableSize),
+			} };
+			const float zeroPoint = blackIndex[1];
 			for (unsigned int i = 0; i < kRGBLookupSize; i++) {
 				/* Apply gamma after gain! */
-				const RGB<float> lutGains = (gains * i / div).min(gammaTableSize - 1);
+				const RGB<float> rawIndex(i / div);
+				const RGB<float> lutGains =
+					(gains * (rawIndex - blackIndex) + zeroPoint)
+						.max(0.0f)
+						.min(gammaTableSize - 1);
 				red[i] = gammaTable_[static_cast<unsigned int>(lutGains.r())];
 				green[i] = gammaTable_[static_cast<unsigned int>(lutGains.g())];
 				blue[i] = gammaTable_[static_cast<unsigned int>(lutGains.b())];
